@meta
id: 31-11
title: Drill — map a key to a function
chapter: 31
type: drill
skills: nvim-lsp, lua-config
par: 96
minutes: 6
reps: 8
vscode: F12, which you did not configure and cannot change easily
open: lsp.lua

@brief
Eight times: add the go-to-definition mapping below the hover mapping, and
save.

    vim.keymap.set('n', 'gd', vim.lsp.buf.definition, { desc = 'Go to definition' })

The point of the rep is the third argument. It is a function, not a string
of keys — no quotes, and no trailing `<CR>`. Putting quotes round it is the
single most common Lua keymap mistake, and it fails by mapping `gd` to
typing the literal text.

Graded in vim. The Lua is written, not run.

@keys
| `vim.lsp.buf.definition` | the function, unquoted |
| `G` `o` | open a line at the bottom |

@hints
Copy the shape of the hover line above it. Only the key and the function
change.
--
No quotes round `vim.lsp.buf.definition`. Quotes would map `gd` to typing
those twenty-two characters into your buffer.

@solution
    G o vim.keymap.set('n', 'gd', vim.lsp.buf.definition, { desc = 'Go to definition' }) <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Eighty-odd keystrokes. What you are drilling is the reflex that a mapping's
right-hand side can be behaviour rather than keystrokes.

For the record, on Neovim 0.11 you may not need this line at all: an
attached buffer already has `grn`, `gra`, `grr`, `gri`, `gO` and `K`
mapped by default, and `gd` follows the tag stack, which the LSP client
also populates. Write your own anyway if `gd` is what your fingers expect
— that is the whole reason keymaps exist.

@start lsp.lua
-- Buffer-local LSP mappings
vim.keymap.set('n', 'K', vim.lsp.buf.hover, { desc = 'Hover documentation' })

@expected lsp.lua
-- Buffer-local LSP mappings
vim.keymap.set('n', 'K', vim.lsp.buf.hover, { desc = 'Hover documentation' })
vim.keymap.set('n', 'gd', vim.lsp.buf.definition, { desc = 'Go to definition' })
